---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';import EveryCut from '../../components/EveryCut.astro';import GuideData from '../../components/GuideData.astro';
import {packDigitalPayload} from '../../dossiers/digital-access.payload.js';
import {bySlug,kicker} from '../../dossiers/registry.js';import {dateStamp} from '../../dossiers/display.js';import {digitalModel} from '../../dossiers/digital-access.model.js';import {digitalFormat} from '../../dossiers/digital-access.format.js';
const D=bySlug['digital-access'],model=digitalModel(),inputs=model.manifest.bundles.map(b=>b.slug).join(', ');
const anatomy=model.plates.filter(p=>p.vary!=='state'),geography=model.plates.filter(p=>p.vary==='state');
const payload=packDigitalPayload(model);
const groups=rows=>[...new Set(rows.map(p=>p.group))];

---
<Base title="Digital access and skills · India Atlas" description={D.description} nav="guides" dateStamp={dateStamp(D)} crumbLeft={`<a href="/guides/">Guides</a> / Households / <b>Digital access</b>`} crumbRight={`${D.statusNote}, <b>${D.version}</b>`} canonical="https://atlas.thisindianlife.today/guides/digital-access/" ogImage="/og/digital-access.png">
<div class="page">
<div class="titleblock break" data-inputs="digital-access-cmst2025-18, digital-access-cmst2025-5, digital-access-cmst2025-3, digital-access-cmst2025-13" data-prose="evergreen and data-bound facts"><div class="kicker">{kicker(D)}</div><div class="hero-grid"><h1 set:html={D.headline}/><p class="lede">A connection at home can serve several people. A person can borrow a phone, go online elsewhere or need help to complete a task. The National Statistical Office’s Comprehensive Modular Survey: Telecom measures these different kinds of access, use and ability.</p></div><div class="meta"><span>Survey <b>{model.plates[0].period}</b></span><span>Population <b>Households and people, with age-specific measures</b></span><span>Instrument <b>National Sample Survey telecom module</b></span><span>Reading <b>Published survey snapshot</b></span></div>
<div class="numbers"><div class="num"><div class="l">Households with internet within their premises</div><div class="v acc" id="n-householdInternet">–</div><div class="d">All households, both rural and urban</div></div><div class="num"><div class="l">People who recently used the internet</div><div class="v" id="n-personInternet">–</div><div class="d">People aged {model.facts.personInternet.ageGroup.toLowerCase()}</div></div><div class="num"><div class="l">People who own a mobile phone</div><div class="v" id="n-mobileOwnership">–</div><div class="d">People aged {model.facts.mobileOwnership.ageGroup.toLowerCase()}, ownership on interview date</div></div><div class="num"><div class="l">People able to report cybercrime online</div><div class="v" id="n-cyberReporting">–</div><div class="d">Self-reported ability among people aged {model.facts.cyberReporting.ageGroup.toLowerCase()}</div></div></div></div>
<section data-inputs="digital-access-cmst2025-1, digital-access-cmst2025-3, digital-access-cmst2025-5, digital-access-cmst2025-18" data-prose="evergreen"><div class="sec-head"><h2>A connected home can still contain people who cannot go online</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="reading-cols"><div class="reading-col"><p>A shared phone may be available only when its owner is home. A connection may be used by some members while others need help. Household availability and individual use describe different parts of that experience. Their percentages use different groups of people and cannot be subtracted to calculate how many are excluded.</p><p>Sending a file, preparing a document and reporting a fraud require different tasks. The survey asks about each separately. A person who uses the internet is not automatically counted as able to perform every task.</p></div><div class="reading-col"><p>Some questions ask about what a person recently did. Others ask what they say they can do. Neither measures speed, reliability, independence, accessibility or how much the connection costs relative to household income.</p><p>Online purchases and banking modes describe narrower groups. First ask who made a purchase or could perform a banking transaction; then read the types within that group.</p></div></div></section>
<section data-inputs={inputs} data-prose="methodology"><div class="sec-head"><h2>How to read the survey</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="wm-grid"><div><p><b>Keep the counted group visible.</b> Household measures count households. Person measures use the age group named in each plate. Phone-type shares describe phone users or owners; network-type shares describe connected households; banking-mode shares describe people able to bank online. Totals and their components overlap.</p><p><b>Ability is self-reported.</b> Interviewers did not test skills. Creating a presentation, sending an attachment and copying information ask about recent performance. Creating a word-processing document, email, banking and cybercrime reporting ask about ability. A task not performed may not have been needed.</p><p><b>The reference period follows the question.</b> Ownership and household connectivity refer to the interview date. Most recent-use and performed-task questions refer to the months before interview; purchases use a shorter period. These moving windows are not a calendar-quarter activity total.</p><p><b>Read state figures with their uncertainty.</b> The telecom survey was designed for national estimates. State figures use the sample available in each state. Relative standard error compares sampling error with the estimate itself. It is not an error in percentage points, and it does not cover every source of survey error.</p><p><b>Person includes respondents beyond the separately displayed male and female groups.</b> The report includes transgender respondents in Person without publishing a separate estimate. Person therefore need not equal a weighted average of just the two displayed gender groups.</p><p><b>Earlier surveys remain separate.</b> The Multiple Indicator Survey had pandemic-extended fieldwork. CAMS covered a longer survey period and a broader set of subjects. Their snapshots are displayed as different rulers. Broadband, mass-media access, mobile-technology coverage and household internet availability are different measures.</p></div><div class="wm-rail"><aside class="margin-note"><span class="mk">A rate, not a subscriber count</span>Survey people and households are different from telecom subscriptions. Multiple SIMs and shared devices prevent a direct conversion.</aside><aside class="margin-note"><span class="mk">No education cut published here</span>The telecom report and API publish age, gender, rural/urban and state cuts, with question-specific categories. They do not publish an education cross-tab for these indicators.</aside><aside class="margin-note"><span class="mk">A dash is missing</span>The report uses a dash for no sample observation. A true zero remains zero. Neither should be inferred from the other.</aside></div></div></section>
<section id="current-reading" data-inputs="facts-digital-access; all digital-access bundles" data-prose="data-bound snapshot facts"><div class="sec-head"><h2>The survey reading</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="reading-cols"><div class="reading-col"><p>Household internet availability and individual internet use answer different questions. The phone plates separate recent use from ownership, then show the kinds of phones within each counted group. The skills plates keep tasks performed apart from reported ability.</p><p>The household plates show the kinds of connections, reasons for lacking internet and online purchases. Every conditional share names the smaller group behind it.</p></div><div class="reading-col"><p>We do not know from these figures whether service was affordable, reliable or usable without assistance. The survey cannot establish why gender or state differences arose.</p><p>All detailed published joint cuts remain in the series register and downloads. The charts use observed source cells and name the dimensions held fixed. They do not manufacture combinations from separate filter lists.</p></div></div></section>
{[anatomy,geography].map((sectionRows,index)=><section data-inputs={inputs} data-prose="evergreen explanations and data-bound facts"><div class="sec-head"><h2>{index===0?'Access, use and the tasks people can perform':'State differences and the sample behind them'}</h2><span class="line"></span></div>{groups(sectionRows).map(group=><div><h3>{group}</h3>{sectionRows.filter(p=>p.group===group).map(p=><div class="break"><figure id={'cb-'+p.id} data-inputs={p.bundles.join(', ')}><div class="plate-head"><div class="t"><h3>{p.title}</h3><div class="sub">{p.sub}. {p.period}. {p.unit==='pct'?'Per cent of the stated group':p.unit==='pct_rse'?'Relative standard error, per cent':'Households surveyed'}.</div></div><div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id={'pv-'+p.id}>–</div><div class="c">{p.rows[0].name}</div></div></div><div class="frame"><div class="plotzone"><div id={'plot-'+p.id}></div></div><div class="plotfoot"><span class="src">{p.source}. {p.period}. {p.unit==='pct_rse'?'Relative standard error (%)':p.unit==='count'?'Sample households':'Percentage of stated population'}.</span><span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><a href={p.sourceUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a><a href={'/data/'+p.bundle+'.csv'}>Whole file</a></span></div></div><div class="explain"><div class="ev"><span class="mk">How to read this</span><p>{p.explain}</p>{p.vary==='state'&&<p>States are arranged by name. Among the published state estimates in this same slice, the unweighted middle value is <span id={'pm-median-'+p.id}>{digitalFormat(model.plateFacts[p.id].median,p.unit)}</span>. That middle describes states, not the national population. Missing cells remain visible as dashes.</p>}</div>{p.rows.some(r=>r.quality)&&<details open><summary>State sample size and available uncertainty</summary><p>Household sample size covers the wider state survey, not this task’s exact age-specific sample. “Unavailable” means that the report has no directly matching relative-standard-error table for this measure. Zero RSE remains the published rounded value.</p><table><thead><tr><th>State</th><th>Estimate</th><th>Survey households</th><th>Relative standard error</th></tr></thead><tbody>{p.rows.map(r=><tr><td>{r.name}</td><td>{digitalFormat(r.value,r.unit)}</td><td>{digitalFormat(r.quality?.households,'count')}</td><td>{r.quality?.rse===null?'Unavailable':digitalFormat(r.quality?.rse,'pct_rse')}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></details>}</div></figure></div>)}</div>)}</section>)}
<section data-inputs={inputs} data-prose="evergreen"><div class="sec-head"><h2>Common misreadings</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="reading-cols"><div class="reading-col"><p><b>“A home connection means everyone is online.”</b> Members can differ in access, ownership and ability. Household and person percentages have different bases.</p><p><b>“Using a phone means owning a phone.”</b> Use can involve a shared device. Ownership is a separate question on the interview date.</p><p><b>“The smartphone share covers everyone.”</b> The phone-type tables describe recent phone users or owners. Their denominator is narrower.</p><p><b>“Able to bank online means regularly making payments.”</b> Ability, transaction use and transaction value are different questions.</p></div><div class="reading-col"><p><b>“All digital skills were tested.”</b> Respondents reported their ability and activities. The interviewer did not assess proficiency.</p><p><b>“A small state difference proves a real gap.”</b> State estimates need sample size and uncertainty. The survey was designed primarily for national estimates.</p><p><b>“A zero and a missing sample mean the same thing.”</b> They remain distinct in the source and downloads.</p><p><b>“The earlier snapshots form a seamless trend.”</b> The survey periods, questions and populations differ. Their rulers stay separate.</p></div></div></section>
<section data-inputs="digital-access source catalogue, API-page receipts, report tables and reconciliation" data-prose="methodology"><div class="sec-head"><h2>Sources and limits</h2><span class="line"></span></div><p>The telecom guide preserves the complete observed grain of the official API catalogue. Each page total is checked, repeated rows rejected and every estimate reconciled with the official report. The report’s household and person sample sizes, weighting totals and published relative standard errors are separate bundles. Survey-estimated population totals are retained for tracing the rate calculations; the report cautions against using them as absolute population counts.</p><p>Older Multiple Indicator Survey and CAMS cells come from the existing archived source bodies. Their full joint cuts now have a guide destination, while remaining separate from telecom. The all-sector reason distribution for Madhya Pradesh has a source total outside the usual rounding allowance; the API and report agree on those cells. We retain the values rather than adjusting them to force an identity. Report-only statements and sample tables in those older instruments still require their own full transcription; they are recorded as unfinished rather than counted as complete report recovery.</p><p>The national-only table on people able to use the internet but not recently using it has no invented state rows. The report publishes selected grouped reasons rather than every detailed reason asked in the survey schedule. This guide retains the published groups. Schools, health-survey outcomes and international estimates have their own guides and are not counted again as telecom expansion. TRAI subscriber statistics have not been added without a verified source packet.</p><p><a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in/sites/default/files/publication_reports/CMST_report_m.pdf">National Statistical Office: Comprehensive Modular Survey, Telecom</a>. <a href="https://mospi.gov.in/sites/default/files/press_release/Final_press%20release_CMS_T.pdf">Official release</a>. <a href="https://mospi.gov.in/sites/default/files/publication_reports/MultipleIndicatorSurveyinIndiaf.pdf">Earlier Multiple Indicator Survey</a>. <a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in/sites/default/files/publication_reports/CAMS%20Report_October_N.pdf">Earlier CAMS report</a>.</p><p>API data revision: <b id="prov-vintage">Unknown</b>. The API supplies no revision date. Report release and archive capture remain separate fields in every file; neither is substituted for an API revision date.</p></section>
<EveryCut slug="digital-access"/><GuideData slug="digital-access"/>
</div><script is:inline type="application/json" id="digital-access-data" set:html={JSON.stringify(payload).replace(/</g,'\u003c')}/><script>import '../../dossiers/digital-access.client.js';import '../../dossiers/guide.client.js';</script>
</Base>

<style>details table {width:100%;table-layout:fixed;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:.82rem} details th,details td {padding:.3rem;overflow-wrap:anywhere;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #E8EBEF} details p {font-size:.9rem} @media(max-width:700px){details table{font-size:.7rem}details th:first-child{width:38%}}</style>
